        "question_type": "Objective",
        "exam_year": "2003",
        "question_text": "<p>Two similar cylindrical cans have heights of 7cm and 21cm, if the smaller holds 250g of sugar, how many kg of sugar will the larger one hold?</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>- If two solids are similar,&nbsp;the ratio of their volumes is equal to the cube of the ratio of their corresponding sides.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Given: Heights of cylinder&nbsp;&nbsp;--&gt;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;21 : 7 or&nbsp;&nbsp;3 : 1 = \\(\\frac{3}{1}\\)&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; = 3 times height enlargement</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Volume / Capacity of larger cylinder&nbsp;= 3\\(^3\\)&nbsp;\\(\\times\\) 250g&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; = 27&nbsp;\\(\\times\\) 250g = 6,750 or 6.75kg</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
